WEBVTT

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:11.830
<v Ira Glass>MS-13-- President Trump talks about them a lot. He's mentioned them 198 times since he became president according to one database of everything that he said publicly. Here he is in Nashville in May.
<v Ira Glass>MS-13——特朗普总统经常谈论他们。据一个记录他所有公开言论的数据库统计，自他担任总统以来，他已经提到了他们198次。这是他五月份在纳什维尔时的讲话。

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:28.390
<v President Trump>This vicious gang has transformed the once peaceful, beautiful communities that I know so well. I know them all. Into blood stained killing fields. Savagely murdering, raping, and mutilating, their victims.
<v President Trump>这个恶毒的帮派已经把我所熟知的那些曾经宁静、美丽的社区变成了血迹斑斑的杀戮场。我非常了解他们。他们残忍地谋杀、强奸并肢解了他们的受害者。

00:00:28.390 --> 00:01:00.890
<v Ira Glass>The communities he knows well are on Long Island, near where he grew up in Queens. MS-13 is a particularly brutal gang. Originally formed in Los Angeles in the 1980s by immigrants and refugees from Central America. Their weapons of choice tend to be machetes and baseball bats. MS-13 has been especially violent lately on Long Island. Last year the president spoke there at Suffolk County Community College. The audience was filled with law enforcement of various kinds-- immigration and customs officials, FBI agents, members of the local police, in their dress uniforms and white gloves.
<v Ira Glass>他所熟知的社区位于长岛，靠近他长大的皇后区。MS-13是一个极其残暴的帮派，最初由来自中美洲的移民和难民于20世纪80年代在洛杉矶成立。他们惯用的武器通常是砍刀和棒球棍。最近，MS-13在长岛尤为猖獗。去年，总统在萨福克县社区学院发表了讲话。听众席坐满了各种执法人员——移民和海关官员、联邦调查局特工、当地警察，他们都穿着制服、戴着白手套。

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:08.640
<v Ira Glass>And the president praised law enforcement for doing a great job eradicating MS-13-- arresting gang members, throwing them in jail, deporting them.
<v Ira Glass>总统称赞执法部门在铲除MS-13方面做得非常好——逮捕帮派成员、将他们投进监狱、驱逐出境。

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:27.560
<v President Trump>And I want to just tell you all together, right now. And the reason I came, this is the most important sentence to me. On behalf of the American people, I want to say, thank you. Thank you very much. Thank you.
<v President Trump>我现在想对你们所有人说，我之所以来到这里，是因为这一句对我来说最重要的话：我谨代表美国人民，向你们表示感谢。非常感谢你们。谢谢。

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:43.920
<v Ira Glass>At this speech, he shook hands with officers from the Suffolk County Police Department. Our story today is about that police department, and it documents the opposite. It documents them failing repeatedly in fighting MS-13. Particularly when the victims were Latino immigrant kids.
<v Ira Glass>在那次演讲中，他与萨福克县警察局的警官们握了手。我们今天的故事就是关于那个警察局的，但它记录的却是事实的另一面。它记录了他们在一再打击MS-13过程中的失职。特别是在受害者是拉丁裔移民孩子时。

00:01:43.920 --> 00:02:00.410
<v Ira Glass>In the end, there were 18 murders in 16 months. Literally, teenagers were disappearing. And for a long time, the Suffolk County Police did very little to investigate. Made the same mistakes over and over. Missed clues, missed leads. Brushed aside distraught parents who were looking for their kids. Parents who had information that might have been helpful.
<v Ira Glass>最终，在16个月内发生了18起谋杀案。青少年们接二连三地失踪了。很长一段时间里，萨福克县警方在调查方面几乎毫无作为。他们一而再、再而三地犯同样的错误，错过线索，错过方向，把那些寻找失踪孩子、心急如焚的父母打发走，而这些父母原本掌握着可能有用的信息。

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:23.030
<v Ira Glass>Investigative reporter Hannah Dreier from ProPublica, who spent the last year in Suffolk County reporting on MS-13 and the police there. And she's found pervasive problems in how the Suffolk County Police Department treats Latinos in general. Problems which dramatically slowed their response to this MS-13 murder spree. And let's just get right to it. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. Here's Hannah Dreier.
<v Ira Glass>ProPublica的调查记者汉娜·德雷尔（Hannah Dreier）去年一年都在萨福克县报道MS-13和当地警方的情况。她发现萨福克县警察局在对待拉丁裔群体方面普遍存在问题，这些问题极大地减缓了他们对MS-13杀戮狂潮的反应。让我们切入正题。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》，下面是汉娜·德雷尔。

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:43.330
<v Hannah Dreier>The first teenager who was murdered was Miguel Garcia Moran. His family always teased his mom, Carlota, over how over protective she was. When Miguel was a toddler, back in Ecuador, she put chicken wire around their house to keep him from running down the street. When the family got green cards and moved to Suffolk County, she imposed a strict 10 PM curfew.
<v Hannah Dreier>第一个被谋杀的少年是米格尔·加西亚·莫兰。他的家人总是取笑他的母亲卡洛塔太爱操心。米格尔还是个蹒跚学步的孩子时，在厄瓜多尔，她就在房子周围围上铁丝网，防止他跑向街道。当家人拿到绿卡搬到萨福克县后，她强加了严格的晚上10点门禁。

00:02:43.330 --> 00:03:07.890
<v Hannah Dreier>And when he wanted to play video games at a neighbor's house on a cold Friday afternoon, in February of 2016, Carlota drove him there, a few blocks away, even though he had just turned 15 and could have walked over. Carlota got home later that evening around 7:30 with some dinner for Miguel. She called out his name. When he didn't answer, she felt a jolt of alarm. She texted him to ask where he was. No response.
<v Hannah Dreier>2016年2月一个寒冷的周五下午，当他想去邻居家玩电子游戏时，尽管米格尔刚满15岁，本可以走过去，但卡洛塔还是开车送他去了几条街外的地方。那天晚上7点半左右，卡洛塔带着晚餐回到家，喊他的名字。当没人回应时，她感到一阵恐慌。她发短信问他在哪，没有回复。

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:28.880
<v Hannah Dreier>She texted him a third, a fourth, a fifth time. By midnight, Carlota's stomach was clenched with dread. Miguel never failed to let her know where he was. He never missed his curfew. He was even too afraid to go to sleepovers. By 2:00 AM, she couldn't wait any longer. She and her boyfriend, Abraham Chaparro, got in the car and started driving.
<v Hannah Dreier>她发了第三次、第四次、第五次短信。到了午夜，卡洛塔因为恐惧而胃部绞痛。米格尔从不隐瞒自己的行踪，也从不违反门禁。他甚至连去朋友家过夜都不敢。凌晨2点，她再也等不下去了，她和男友亚伯拉罕·查帕罗坐上车，开始四处寻找。

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:33.110
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:35.700
<v Interpreter>And I was like, where's Miguel. What happened to Miguel?
<v Interpreter>我说，米格尔在哪？米格尔发生了什么事？

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.010
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:41.440
<v Interpreter>He normally, he calls me, every 5, every 10 minutes.
<v Interpreter>他通常每5或10分钟就会给我打个电话。

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:50.070
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:03:50.070 --> 00:04:02.660
<v Interpreter>But during all those hours, they seemed just so long, and I didn't know whether I should scream, or cry. I just felt like I was losing the whole world at that moment.
<v Interpreter>但在那几个小时里，每一分钟都显得如此漫长，我不知道我是该尖叫还是该哭。那一刻，我感觉自己仿佛失去了整个世界。

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:09.390
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:12.120
<v Interpreter>At that point, I was just saying, you know, it's like someone stole my son.
<v Interpreter>在那一刻，我一直在说，就像有人偷走了我的儿子。

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:15.010
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:32.510
<v Hannah Dreier>After hours of searching the streets, Carlota finally went home. She sat down on her bed, fully clothed, and waited for the sun to come up, so she and Abraham could go to the police station. When they got there, two Suffolk County Police officers were sitting on an elevated platform, behind the counter, drinking coffee.
<v Hannah Dreier>在街上搜寻了几个小时后，卡洛塔终于回了家。她和衣坐在床上，等待天亮，这样她和亚伯拉罕就能去警察局。他们到达时，两名萨福克县警察正坐在柜台后的高台上喝咖啡。

00:04:32.510 --> 00:05:07.640
<v Hannah Dreier>Carlota only speaks Spanish. But her boyfriend, Abraham, has lived in the US for years, and speaks some English. Abraham helped her give the officers the basics. The missing kid was Miguel Garcia Moran, 15 years old, tall, and big, 235 pounds, lived in the town of Brentwood, last seen at a friend's house the night before. A detective named Luis Perez was assigned to the case. He's a veteran detective in Brentwood, and one of the few officers on the force who speaks Spanish. The police told Carlota and Abraham that some officers were going to drive around their neighborhood to look for Miguel. But really, they shouldn't be too concerned.
<v Hannah Dreier>卡洛塔只会说西班牙语，但她的男友亚伯拉罕在美国住过几年，会说一点英语。亚伯拉罕帮她向警官提供了基本情况：失踪者是15岁的米格尔·加西亚·莫兰，个子很高，体重235磅，住在布伦特伍德镇，最后一次露面是在前一天晚上朋友家里。一位名叫路易斯·佩雷斯的探员被分派到此案。他是布伦特伍德的一名资深探员，也是警局里为数不多会说西班牙语的警官之一。警方告诉卡洛塔和亚伯拉罕，会有警员开车在社区周围寻找米格尔，但他们其实不必太过担心。

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:13.000
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:24.770
<v Interpreter>And they said, look, it's Saturday. Your son's going to be back on Monday. He's probably just hanging out with friends. But I said, no, my son would never do that. He always comes home early. This isn't like him.
<v Interpreter>他们说，看，现在是周六。你儿子周一就会回来。他可能只是和朋友出去了。但我说，不，我儿子绝不会那样做。他总是很早就回家，这不像他的作风。

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:47.450
<v Hannah Dreier>At this point, Miguel had been missing for 24 hours, which in a missing persons case are the most crucial. There's a truism in law enforcement-- the first 24 hours are the only 24 hours. The New York City Police Department has a checklist of dozens of things officers have to do immediately if a minor goes missing. Things like, talking to the kids' friends, checking their social media accounts, and putting out a press release.
<v Hannah Dreier>此时，米格尔已经失踪了24小时，这在失踪人口案件中是最关键的时间。执法部门有一句格言——前24小时是唯一的24小时。纽约市警察局有一份清单，列出了如果未成年人失踪，警官必须立即做的几十件事，比如与孩子的同学们交谈、检查他们的社交媒体账号，以及发布新闻稿。

00:05:47.450 --> 00:06:21.410
<v Hannah Dreier>Nassau County, which borders Suffolk County on Long Island, has an even more extensive protocol, which includes alerting state officials within two hours of taking a report. The Suffolk County Police handbook, on the other hand, has no checklist for what to do if a minor goes missing. Nothing about talking to the kid's friends. Nothing about social media. Nothing about a press release. In the whole handbook, there are basically two paragraphs about what to do if a minor is reported missing. And they boil down to take a report, search the area where they live. That's it.
<v Hannah Dreier>长岛上与萨福克县接壤的拿骚县，其规程更为详尽，其中包括在接到报案后两小时内向州政府官员发出预警。而萨福克县警方的手册里，根本没有关于未成年人失踪该如何操作的清单。没有关于与孩子同学交谈的内容，没有社交媒体相关信息，也没有发布新闻稿的说明。整本手册里，关于报案后该做什么只有两段话，总结起来就是：做笔录，搜查居住区域。就这样。

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:50.280
<v Hannah Dreier>So on Sunday, two days after Miguel went missing, that's what the Suffolk County Police did. They searched the woods closest to Carlota's home. And they also talked to the neighbors. On Monday, three days after Miguel went missing, the police put out a press release and a photo of Miguel in a green shirt with a red bandanna around his head. It said, quote, "Detectives do not believe there is foul play involved in Moran's disappearance." And they listed Miguel as a runaway, which Carlota found extra upsetting.
<v Hannah Dreier>所以周日，在米格尔失踪两天后，萨福克县警方就是这么做的。他们搜查了离卡洛塔家最近的树林，也询问了邻居。周一，即米格尔失踪三天后，警方发布了一份新闻稿，附上了米格尔身穿绿衬衫、头上系着红头巾的照片。稿件称，引用：“侦探们认为莫兰的失踪不涉及刑事犯罪。”他们将米格尔列为离家出走者，这让卡洛塔感到格外痛苦。

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:53.270
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:06:53.270 --> 00:07:02.150
<v Interpreter>I felt a lot of indignation because they would say that, oh, he just ran away from home. And I'd think, why would you say that?
<v Interpreter>我感到非常愤怒，因为他们那样说，噢，他只是离家出走了。我会想，你们凭什么这么说？

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:06.620
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:16.960
<v Interpreter>Why would you say that he ran away from home when I have proof? I have pictures of us, like, going to the store. Us going to different places, even from that day, where we went to the firefighters.
<v Interpreter>我明明有证据，为什么你们要说他是离家出走的？我有我们一起去商店、去不同地方的照片，甚至那天我们还一起去了消防站。

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:34.450
<v Hannah Dreier>The day Miguel disappeared, he and Carlota had gone to the firehouse to fill out an application. He wanted to join the volunteer firefighters. They spent the morning and afternoon together as they often did on weekends. Miguel liked to walk with his arms slung around his mother's shoulders. At 5' 10", he towered over her.
<v Hannah Dreier>米格尔失踪那天，他和卡洛塔去消防站填了一份申请表，他想加入志愿消防员。他们像往常的周末一样一起度过了上午和下午。米格尔喜欢手臂搭在母亲的肩膀上走路。他身高5英尺10英寸，比她高出一大截。

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:54.890
<v Hannah Dreier>Miguel was a total mama's boy. For his 15th birthday, a few months earlier, his older sister helped him get Carlota's name tattooed on his arm. So to Carlota, the thought that Miguel had run away from home was insane. She tried to explain this to the police, but they still listed him as a runaway. And when they did that, the police were acting against guidelines from multiple agencies.
<v Hannah Dreier>米格尔是个典型的“妈宝”。几个月前他15岁生日时，姐姐帮他在手臂上纹上了卡洛塔的名字。所以对卡洛塔来说，米格尔离家出走的想法是荒谬的。她试图向警方解释这一点，但他们仍然把他列为离家出走。这样做时，警方实际上违背了多个机构的指导方针。

00:07:54.890 --> 00:08:21.180
<v Hannah Dreier>According to the National Center for Missing and Exploited Children, and the Department of Justice, law enforcement should quote, "Assume the child is at risk, until investigative facts contradict that assumption." When a kid is listed as a runaway instead of as a missing or endangered person, it basically stops an investigation before it starts. Listing Miguel as a runaway is the first big mistake the police make. They'll be many more.
<v Hannah Dreier>根据国家失踪与受剥削儿童中心和司法部的说法，执法部门应当“假设孩子处于危险之中，直到调查事实反驳这一假设”。当一个孩子被列为离家出走而不是失踪或处于危险状态的人时，这基本上在调查开始之前就将其扼杀了。将米格尔列为离家出走是警方犯下的第一个重大错误。而他们之后还会犯更多错误。

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:24.600
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:32.500
<v Interpreter>My hope, truthfully, was that if I go to the police they would help us. You know, this was just a disaster. A disaster.
<v Interpreter>说实话，我当初的希望是，如果我们去报警，他们能帮助我们。你知道，这简直是一场灾难。一场灾难。

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:33.990
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:36.350
<v Interpreter>Going to the police was a disaster.
<v Interpreter>报警成了一场灾难。

00:08:36.350 --> 00:09:02.990
<v Hannah Dreier>After days with no apparent progress from police, Carlota, realized she needed a new strategy. She started going around posting missing person flyers, which she wrote herself because the police had not made any. She searched for Miguel every day on residential streets, strip malls, at American Eagle, his favorite clothing store, and in the woods. Including one patch of forest that the police call the killing fields. It's a gang hang out and dumping ground for bodies.
<v Hannah Dreier>在警方明显没有取得进展几天后，卡洛塔意识到她需要一个新的策略。她开始到处张贴失踪人员传单，因为警方从未制作过。她每天在住宅区、露天购物中心、米格尔最喜欢的服装店美国鹰（American Eagle）和树林里寻找米格尔。其中包括一片警察称为“杀戮场”的林区。那里是帮派聚集地和尸体处理场所。

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:25.770
<v Hannah Dreier>The trees are so dense there that it's hard to see very far into them. Condoms and cigar wrappers and empty spray paint cans blanket the ground. At the heart of these woods is an abandoned psychiatric hospital. The dozens of possibilities she'd been replaying in her head, we're converging on a single fear, MS-13. She knew they were active in Brentwood, but she didn't know much more.
<v Hannah Dreier>那里的树木茂密，很难看清远处。避孕套、雪茄包装纸和空喷漆罐铺满了地面。这片树林中央是一所废弃的精神病院。她在脑海中反复回想的几十种可能性，正汇聚成一个单一的恐惧：MS-13。她知道他们在布伦特伍德很活跃，但了解的并不多。

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:53.090
<v Hannah Dreier>MS-13 leaders in the US are often really young, like high school aged. On Long Island, some of the most brutal MS-13 violence has been masterminded by 15 and 16-year-olds. Brentwood high school was shared by a few MS-13 cliques. That's what they call their subgroups-- the Hollywood Locos, the Brentwood Locos, and the Sailors. They wear blue plastic rosaries and Nike Cortez sneakers. The other gangs at the school were as color coded as teams at camp.
<v Hannah Dreier>美国境内的MS-13头目通常非常年轻，像高中生一样。在长岛，一些最残暴的MS-13暴力事件是由15、16岁的青少年策划的。布伦特伍德高中里有几个MS-13分支——这就是他们对自己小组的称呼，分别是Hollywood Locos、Brentwood Locos和Sailors。他们戴着蓝色塑料念珠，穿着耐克Cortez运动鞋。学校里的其他帮派也像夏令营里的队伍一样，通过衣服颜色进行区分。

00:09:53.090 --> 00:10:19.200
<v Hannah Dreier>At the time Miguel went missing, Suffolk County Police mostly left the gangs alone. Members had the run of the woods around town and the halls of the high school. Miguel's older sister, Lady, was a sophomore at Brentwood high school when Miguel went missing. Lady has a habit of narrowing her eyes when she talks. She's streetwise, but also churchgoing. She had been a natural loner all her life, unlike Miguel, who has always been teased for his stutter, and was desperate to seem cool.
<v Hannah Dreier>米格尔失踪时，萨福克县警方基本上放任这些帮派不管。成员们在镇上的树林和高中走廊里横行霸道。米格尔的姐姐莱迪在米格尔失踪时是布伦特伍德高中的二年级学生。莱迪说话时习惯眯起眼睛。她很懂世故，但也经常去教堂。她一生中大部分时间都是一个天生的独行侠，不像米格尔，他总是因为口吃被取笑，拼命想显得酷一点。

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:29.910
<v Hannah Dreier>At Brentwood high school, Lady, had become even more standoffish to keep the gang kids away, which wasn't always easy because gang members aggressively approach new students at school and pressure them to join.
<v Hannah Dreier>在布伦特伍德高中，为了让帮派孩子远离自己，莱迪变得更加疏离，但这并不容易，因为帮派成员会在学校积极接触新生，并强迫他们加入。

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:34.130
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:42.560
<v Interpreter>If they didn't see you with a stern look in your face, or if they didn't see you mad, then they would treat you badly.
<v Interpreter>如果他们没在你脸上看到严肃的表情，或者没看到你生气，他们就会粗暴地对待你。

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:45.730
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:56.780
<v Interpreter>It was different for me because I had a strong temper. But Miguel was not like that. He was weak. He was the spoiled child.
<v Interpreter>对我来说情况不同，因为我的脾气很硬。但米格尔不是那样的，他很软弱，他是被宠坏的孩子。

00:10:56.780 --> 00:11:00.200
<v Hannah Dreier>When Miguel started in the high school, did you tell him the things that you had learned?
<v Hannah Dreier>当米格尔开始上高中时，你告诉过他你学到的那些事情吗？

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:04.460
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:13.170
<v Interpreter>I would tell him, you know, when you're changing period from one class to the other, don't talk to certain people. And he'd say, OK.
<v Interpreter>我会告诉他，你知道，当你从一门课换到另一门课时，不要和某些人说话。他说，好的。

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:16.040
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:28.490
<v Interpreter>And at first, he did. He did listen to me. But then, you know, he had friends that I did not know. And I told him, do not make friends.
<v Interpreter>起初他确实听了我的。但后来，你知道，他有了我不认识的朋友。我告诉他，不要交朋友。

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:32.190
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:38.420
<v Hannah Dreier>A week after Miguel went missing, Carlota did an interview with a Spanish language news channel to talk about his disappearance.
<v Hannah Dreier>米格尔失踪一周后，卡洛塔接受了一家西班牙语新闻频道的采访，谈论他的失踪。

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:41.130
<v Radio Announcer>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Radio Announcer>[说西班牙语]

00:11:41.130 --> 00:12:10.010
<v Hannah Dreier>The footage shows Carlota leaning heavily on Abraham, as the two walk through a strip mall in jackets and winter hats, taping up their missing person flyers. She says, there are so many things you hear about in this country. That the gangs go and take kids. That's what I fear the most. That they've taken him. Carlota brought up the possibility that gangs might be involved in Miguel's disappearance. But the Suffolk County Police still said no foul play was suspected and continued to list him as a runaway.
<v Hannah Dreier>录像显示，卡洛塔沉重地依靠着亚伯拉罕，两人穿着夹克和冬帽走在露天购物中心贴失踪人员传单。她说：“在这个国家你听说过很多事情，比如帮派会去抢孩子。这是我最害怕的，他们把他带走了。”卡洛塔提到了帮派可能参与米格尔失踪的可能性。但萨福克县警方仍然称没有可疑迹象，并继续将他列为离家出走。

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:31.070
<v Hannah Dreier>Another big problem with the police's treatment of these immigrant families, was they were dismissive, and sometimes even treated them like suspects. Carlota came to feel intimidated by Detective Perez. She says he seemed angry at her. He was big, like a bodybuilder, with tattoos circling his arms. One day he sat her down and accused her of knowing more than she was saying about Miguel.
<v Hannah Dreier>警方对待这些移民家庭的另一个大问题是他们的冷漠，有时甚至把他们当作嫌疑人。卡洛塔开始感到被佩雷斯探员恐吓。她说他似乎对她很生气。他身材魁梧，像个健美运动员，双臂纹着纹身。一天，他让她坐下，指责她对米格尔的事情隐瞒了实情。

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:33.760
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:42.120
<v Interpreter>Then he started telling me, you know where he is. And I said, what? Like, why do I know where he is? I come here all the time asking where he is.
<v Interpreter>然后他开始对我说，你知道他在哪。我说，什么？为什么我知道他在哪？我一直来这问他在哪。

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:43.250
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:50.940
<v Interpreter>If I knew where he was then I wouldn't be here. And I just wanted to hit him when he told me that. And he said, well, if you don't know where he is--
<v Interpreter>如果我知道他在哪，我就不会在这了。当他说那话时，我真想打他。他说，好吧，如果你不知道他在哪——

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:51.930
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:56.360
<v Interpreter>--then you should go to a witch doctor, because then, they can help you find Miguel.
<v Interpreter>——那你应该去找个巫医，因为那样他们能帮你找到米格尔。

00:12:56.360 --> 00:13:02.060
<v Hannah Dreier>Detective Perez told Carlota to find a brujo, which is Spanish for witch doctor, or fortune teller.
<v Hannah Dreier>佩雷斯探员告诉卡洛塔去找个巫医（brujo）。

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:03.890
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:14.250
<v Interpreter>I don't know why he said that. It's like, I can't do anything more for you. You need to go and see the witch doctor because he just didn't want to help anymore.
<v Interpreter>我不知道他为什么要那样说。感觉就是，我不能再为你做什么了。你需要去找巫医，因为他只是不想再帮忙了。

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:17.320
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:32.650
<v Hannah Dreier>One of the starkest ways that the police demonstrated their indifference to the families, was that they wouldn't even speak to them in their own language. This underlies so much of what went wrong. Even Detective Perez, who could speak Spanish. Lady told me something else that happened in that brujo conversation.
<v Hannah Dreier>警方表现出对这些家庭冷漠的最显著方式之一，就是他们甚至不愿用对方的语言与他们交谈。这构成了许多错误的基础。即使是会说西班牙语的佩雷斯探员也是如此。莱迪告诉我关于那次巫医对话中发生的另一件事。

00:13:32.650 --> 00:13:37.400
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:47.570
<v Interpreter>He just said, I'm not speaking Spanish anymore because this is America. Nobody speaks Spanish, only English, so why don't you just go back.
<v Interpreter>他说，我不再说西班牙语了，因为这里是美国。没人说西班牙语，只说英语，所以你们为什么不滚回去。

00:13:47.570 --> 00:13:48.370
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:52.840
<v Interpreter>Even knowing that my mom doesn't speak a word of English.
<v Interpreter>即使他知道我妈妈一句英语也不会说。

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:54.010
<v Hannah Dreier>How did you feel when he said that?
<v Hannah Dreier>当他说那话时，你有什么感觉？

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:56.650
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:13:56.650 --> 00:14:09.370
<v Interpreter>I felt bad. You know, because she was Latino, and to come and say this to us knowing that we needed him. That we needed for him to find someone for us.
<v Interpreter>我感觉很糟糕。因为她是拉丁裔，在明知我们需要他、需要他为我们找到人的时候，他却来对我们说这种话。

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:11.380
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:22.430
<v Interpreter>And he just simply didn't feel like speaking our language to us.
<v Interpreter>而他只是单纯地不想对我们说我们的语言。

00:14:22.430 --> 00:14:48.070
<v Hannah Dreier>Carlota and Lady are not unusual in Suffolk County. Though the county includes really ritzy places like the Hamptons, it also has towns like Brentwood, where families get by on minimum wage jobs, and most people speak Spanish as their first language. 20% of the county is Hispanic. But in 2016, in the entire Suffolk County Police Department, 3,500 employees, only three people were certified to interpret for Spanish speakers.
<v Hannah Dreier>卡洛塔和莱迪在萨福克县并不罕见。尽管该县包括像汉普顿这样非常奢华的地方，但它也有布伦特伍德这样的城镇，家庭依靠最低工资工作生活，大多数人以西班牙语为第一语言。该县20%的人口是西班牙裔。但在2016年，在拥有3500名员工的整个萨福克县警察局，只有3人获得了西班牙语口译认证。

00:14:48.070 --> 00:15:18.100
<v Hannah Dreier>Unlike a majority of big police departments, Suffolk County doesn't give officers extra pay for knowing a second language. They do pay police well, though. They're actually one of the best paid departments in the country. Detective Perez made just under $200,000 a year, and lived in a gated community several towns away from where he works. Detective Perez wouldn't talk to me for this story, by the way. When I knocked on his door, he said he had no comment, and told me to get off his property.
<v Hannah Dreier>与大多数大型警察部门不同，萨福克县不会因为警官会第二语言而给予额外报酬。不过，他们确实给警察支付高额薪水。他们实际上是全国待遇最好的部门之一。佩雷斯探员一年收入略低于20万美元，住在离他工作地点几个镇远的一个门禁社区。顺便说一句，佩雷斯探员不愿接受我的采访。当我敲他的门时，他说没什么可评论的，并叫我离开他的私人领地。

00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:41.040
<v Hannah Dreier>Almost two months after Miguel went missing, there was a breakthrough in the case. But the person who made it was not a Suffolk County Detective. It was Miguel's sister, Lady. One morning, she borrowed Abraham's cell phone and discovered that Miguel had left his Facebook account open there. She suddenly had access to his Facebook Messenger chats. She could look through all the conversations he had with his friends, up to the day he went missing.
<v Hannah Dreier>米格尔失踪近两个月后，案件取得了突破。但做出突破的人不是萨福克县的侦探，而是米格尔的姐姐莱迪。一天早上，她借用了亚伯拉罕的手机，发现米格尔在那里登录着Facebook账号。她突然可以访问他的Facebook Messenger聊天记录。她可以查看他与朋友之间的所有对话，一直到他失踪的那天。

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:42.090
<v Lady>Here, look.
<v Lady>你看，在这里。

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:45.790
<v Hannah Dreier>Lady showed me the messages.
<v Hannah Dreier>莱迪向我展示了这些信息。

00:15:45.790 --> 00:15:49.170
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:54.510
<v Interpreter>Yes. That was Miguel's entire messenger conversation.
<v Interpreter>是的，那就是米格尔完整的Messenger对话。

00:15:54.510 --> 00:16:25.300
<v Hannah Dreier>Most of them were failed attempts to flirt with girls at school. In others, he's talking about smoking weed. In one conversation, he tells a friend he wants a pair of Nike Cortez sneakers. But the friend tells him he can't wear those because they're a sign of MS-13. That's an important piece of advice. MS-13 doesn't have businesses like a normal criminal organization. And instead, commits violence for the sake of violence, killing victims with bats and machetes for minor shows of disrespect, like wearing Nike Cortez shoes without their permission.
<v Hannah Dreier>其中大部分是与学校女生调情失败的尝试。在其他对话中，他在谈论吸大麻。在一场对话中，他告诉一个朋友他想要一双耐克Cortez运动鞋。但朋友告诉他不能穿那个，因为那是MS-13的标志。这是一个重要的建议。MS-13不像正常的犯罪组织那样有商业往来，而是为了暴力而实施暴力，仅仅因为轻微的挑衅，比如未经允许穿了耐克Cortez鞋，就用棒球棍和砍刀杀害受害者。

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:50.910
<v Hannah Dreier>And then, there's just one conversation from the day Miguel disappeared. Text and audio messages between Miguel and someone named Alexander Lokote. Lokote isn't a last name. It means something like homeboy or gangster. So you can see the day that Miguel went missing. There's like 20 messages back and forth between him and Alexander.
<v Hannah Dreier>然后，还有关于米格尔失踪当天的唯一一段对话。米格尔和一个叫“亚历山大·洛科特（Alexander Lokote）”的人之间的文字和语音信息。洛科特不是姓氏，它的意思大约是“老乡”或“黑帮成员”。所以你可以看到米格尔失踪的那一天，他和亚历山大之间有大约20条往来信息。

00:16:50.910 --> 00:17:17.590
<v Hannah Dreier>The conversation begins at 8:54 in the morning. And ends at 7:06 that night. Right around when Carlota was getting home and discovering that Miguel wasn't there. Alexander tells Miguel he's gotten his hands on some weed, and invites him to smoke later on with two other boys. One of the other boys is a kid named Jairo. Miguel had just started smoking weed. His mother and sister didn't even know he had tried it. Miguel seems pleased and surprised, and responds with a voice message.
<v Hannah Dreier>对话开始于上午8:54，结束于那天晚上7:06，就在卡洛塔回家并发现米格尔不在家的时候。亚历山大告诉米格尔他搞到了一些大麻，邀请他稍后和另外两个男孩一起抽。其中一个男孩叫杰罗。米格尔刚开始吸大麻，他的母亲和姐姐甚至不知道他试过这个。米格尔看起来很高兴也很惊讶，并回复了一条语音信息。

00:17:17.590 --> 00:17:21.500
<v Miguel>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Miguel>[说西班牙语]

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:33.560
<v Hannah Dreier>That's Miguel. He's saying, oh, you're so bad. What have you done? At this point in the morning, Miguel was still with Carlota at the mall. It sounds like her in the background of some of these messages, talking and laughing.
<v Hannah Dreier>那是米格尔。他说，噢，你太坏了。你干了什么？在那天的那个时候，米格尔还和卡洛塔在购物中心。背景里听起来像是她，在说话和笑。

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:37.990
<v Miguel>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Miguel>[说西班牙语]

00:17:37.990 --> 00:18:06.120
<v Hannah Dreier>Miguel says he wants to bring along a friend, but Alexander says, no, don't do that. Jairo won't like it. Jairo's going to hook them up with weed. Later, Miguel and Alexander argue about whether to meet in the woods or at Miguel's house. Miguel tells Alexander that he'd prefer to meet at his house because he doesn't like to go out into the trees. But finally, Miguel gives in and agrees to meet in the woods near the high school, a few blocks away from his house. The last message is from Alexander.
<v Hannah Dreier>米格尔说他想带一个朋友，但亚历山大说，不，别那样做。杰罗会不喜欢的。杰罗会帮他们搞到大麻。后来，米格尔和亚历山大争论是在树林里见面还是在米格尔家里。米格尔告诉亚历山大他更愿意在家里见面，因为他不喜欢去树林里。但最终，米格尔屈服了，同意在靠近高中、离他家几个街区的树林里见面。最后一条信息是亚历山大发的。

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:12.720
<v Alexander>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Alexander>[说西班牙语]

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:40.220
<v Hannah Dreier>He tells Miguel he's by the fluorescent lights already, at the school. He seems annoyed that Miguel isn't there yet. And then, nothing. In the days and months after that night, Miguel's Facebook account is flooded with messages from family and friends wondering where he is, or vowing to find him. But none of those messages was from Alexander Lokote.
<v Hannah Dreier>他告诉米格尔他已经在学校的荧光灯旁了。他似乎对米格尔还没到感到恼火。然后，就没有然后了。在那天晚上之后的日子和几个月里，米格尔的Facebook账号被家人和朋友询问他下落或誓要找到他的信息淹没。但这些信息中没有一条来自亚历山大·洛科特。

00:18:40.220 --> 00:19:06.920
<v Hannah Dreier>Abraham showed all these Facebook messages to Detective Perez. Carlota said soon afterward, the detective called with a strange suggestion. He asked Abraham and Carlota if they wanted to meet him at the high school. He said he was going to interview Alexander Lokote in the principal's office, to see what he might know. And for some reason, he wanted to give them a chance to be there for it. When they got to the high school, Alexander Lokote was there. Lady had been called out of class, and was waiting outside the office.
<v Hannah Dreier>亚伯拉罕把这些Facebook信息都给了佩雷斯探员。卡洛塔说，不久之后，侦探打来电话，提出了一个奇怪的建议。他问亚伯拉罕和卡洛塔是否想在高中和他见面。他说他要在校长办公室面试亚历山大·洛科特，看看他可能知道些什么。不知为何，他想给他们一个机会在现场。当他们到达高中时，亚历山大·洛科特就在那。莱迪被从课堂叫出来，在办公室外等待。

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:08.800
<v Hannah Dreier>What did Alexander look like?
<v Hannah Dreier>亚历山大长什么样？

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:10.790
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:19:10.790 --> 00:19:13.280
<v Interpreter>He was scared.
<v Interpreter>他很害怕。

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:14.760
<v Hannah Dreier>Scared how?
<v Hannah Dreier>怎么个害怕法？

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:16.310
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:19:16.310 --> 00:19:22.640
<v Interpreter>He looked kind of pale, and as though he wanted to get out of there right away.
<v Interpreter>他看起来脸色发白，好像想立刻逃离那里。

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:25.880
<v Hannah Dreier>Did Alexander dress like a gang member?
<v Hannah Dreier>亚历山大穿得像帮派成员吗？

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:28.350
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:33.260
<v Interpreter>Yeah, he was dressed like a gang member. He was wearing his white long t-shirt.
<v Interpreter>是，他穿得像帮派成员。他穿着那件白色长T恤。

00:19:33.260 --> 00:19:35.570
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:19:35.570 --> 00:19:38.080
<v Interpreter>And I saw the rosary.
<v Interpreter>我还看到了念珠。

00:19:38.080 --> 00:19:39.490
<v Hannah Dreier>What color rosary?
<v Hannah Dreier>什么颜色的念珠？

00:19:39.490 --> 00:19:41.360
<v Lady>Blue.
<v Lady>蓝色。

00:19:41.360 --> 00:20:03.990
<v Hannah Dreier>I ran this episode past a few police experts. And they all said it was really odd that Detective Perez invited the victim's family to an interrogation of a witness or a potential suspect like that. For one thing, it could make it harder to use anything Alexander said that day to build the case in court. They said that if Detective Perez suspected foul play at that point, he should have gotten a warrant to search Alexander's phone.
<v Hannah Dreier>我把这一集内容拿给几位警察专家看。他们都说佩雷斯探员邀请受害者家属参加对证人或潜在嫌疑人的审讯真的很奇怪。一方面，这可能使在法庭上使用亚历山大那天说的任何话来建立案件变得更加困难。他们说，如果佩雷斯探员当时怀疑有刑事犯罪，他本应申请搜查亚历山大手机的搜查令。

00:20:03.990 --> 00:20:21.990
<v Hannah Dreier>Abraham thought that Alexander looked young, and so weak that he couldn't break a plate. He said that Alexander told him that he, Jairo, and another kid, had planned to go with Miguel to some train tracks. But Miguel never showed up. Carlota told Abraham that so far, her teenage daughter seemed to have made more progress than the detectives.
<v Hannah Dreier>亚伯拉罕认为亚历山大看起来很年轻，虚弱得连个盘子都摔不碎。他说亚历山大告诉他，他和杰罗以及另一个孩子原计划和米格尔去铁轨附近，但米格尔从未出现。卡洛塔告诉亚伯拉罕，到目前为止，她十几岁的女儿似乎比侦探们取得了更大的进展。

00:20:21.990 --> 00:20:24.360
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:33.030
<v Interpreter>This was such a key thing that he knows. What else do they want? They weren't doing anything.
<v Interpreter>这是他知道的关键线索，他们还需要什么？他们什么都没做。

00:20:33.030 --> 00:20:36.010
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:54.410
<v Hannah Dreier>It's not clear what happened to Alexander Lokote. Lady tells me that after this meeting, she stopped seeing him in school. I've been trying to find Alexander for months, but nobody I've talked to knows his real last name.
<v Hannah Dreier>不清楚亚历山大·洛科特发生了什么。莱迪告诉我，在那次会议后，她就没在学校见过他。我已经试着找亚历山大好几个月了，但我谈过的每个人都不知道他真正的姓氏。

00:20:54.410 --> 00:21:07.010
<v Hannah Dreier>Carlota stopped going to her job at an envelope factory. She spent most of her time in her basement apartment looking through Miguel's clothes, breathing in their smell, and trying to distract herself by reading romance novels.
<v Hannah Dreier>卡洛塔停止了在信封厂的工作。她大部分时间都待在地下室公寓里，翻看米格尔的衣服，呼吸它们上面的味道，并试图通过阅读浪漫小说来分散自己的注意力。

00:21:07.010 --> 00:21:26.460
<v Hannah Dreier>She hated when Lady went out, even to attend church. Nearly three months after Miguel disappeared, at the end of April, the state finally gave Carlota a missing person poster. The poster read, quote, "Miguel is a runaway." Then one afternoon in May, Carlota was watching the news at home when she saw another mother on TV, crying.
<v Hannah Dreier>她讨厌莱迪出门，哪怕是去教堂。米格尔失踪近三个月后，4月底，州政府终于给了卡洛塔一张失踪人员海报。海报上写着，引用：“米格尔是离家出走。”然后5月的一个下午，卡洛塔在家里看新闻时，看到电视上另一位母亲在哭泣。

