<v Hannah Dreier>不，那个报案人深信他不是离家出走。

00:38:03.660 --> 00:38:26.140
<v Timothy Sini>I don't know the source of the runaway component. I don't know where the runaway part came from. I don't know if it came from someone in the family. I don't know if it came from another witness. I don't know if it came from a friend of the boy. But there is no question that, internally, there's no doubt that the Suffolk County Police Department, essentially out of the gate, believed that there was foul play.
<v Timothy Sini>我不知道离家出走部分的消息来源。我不知道离家出走的部分来自哪里。我不知道是来自家人，还是来自其他证人，或者是男孩的朋友。但毫无疑问，在内部，萨福克县警察局从一开始就认为发生了刑事犯罪。

00:38:26.140 --> 00:38:37.370
<v Hannah Dreier>This is, of course, at odds with the press release the Suffolk County Police put out after Miguel disappeared, which said, quote, "Detectives do not believe there is foul play involved in Moran's disappearance."
<v Hannah Dreier>这当然与萨福克县警方在米格尔失踪后发布的新闻稿相矛盾，该稿件称，引用：“侦探们不认为莫兰的失踪涉及刑事犯罪。”

00:38:37.370 --> 00:38:57.640
<v Hannah Dreier>Timothy Sini said he wasn't aware of any policy about listing a kid as a runaway instead of as a missing person. I wanted to know why the Suffolk County Police Department's rules and procedures had so little to say about what to do if a kid goes missing. Just two paragraphs compared to much more detailed guidelines and checklists in Nassau County and New York City.
<v Hannah Dreier>蒂莫西·西尼说他不知道有什么政策将孩子列为离家出走而不是失踪人口。我想知道为什么萨福克县警察局的规则和程序对孩子失踪该怎么做提及得如此之少。与拿骚县和纽约市更详细的指导方针和清单相比，只有两段话。

00:38:57.640 --> 00:39:03.130
<v Hannah Dreier>Former Commissioner Sini said he didn't know what I was talking about, so I showed him a copy of that section, which I had with me.
<v Hannah Dreier>前局长西尼说他不知道我在说什么，所以我给他看了那部分的副本，我随身带着。

00:39:03.130 --> 00:39:05.080
<v Hannah Dreier>Basically, there are two paragraphs. One says--
<v Hannah Dreier>基本上有两段。一段说——

00:39:05.080 --> 00:39:13.840
<v Timothy Sini>I understand what you said. And I said I think there is actually more to it. And I think maybe, depending-- I don't know if we can get that to you, because that would essentially reveal investigative steps, but--
<v Timothy Sini>我理解你说的。我认为实际上还有更多内容。我想可能，根据——我不知道我们能否把它给你，因为那本质上会揭露调查步骤，但——

00:39:13.840 --> 00:39:16.040
<v Hannah Dreier>There's more to it? Like, more in the rules and procedures?
<v Hannah Dreier>还有更多内容？比如，规则和程序里有更多内容？

00:39:16.040 --> 00:39:16.480
<v Timothy Sini>Yes, I believe there--
<v Timothy Sini>是的，我相信有——

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:19.010
<v Hannah Dreier>I mean, these are the rules and procedures.
<v Hannah Dreier>我的意思是，这就是规则和程序。

00:39:19.010 --> 00:39:28.360
<v Timothy Sini>I can assure you that these are not the rules and procedures of the Suffolk County Police Department. The rules the procedures of the Suffolk County Police Department are about 5,000 pages long.
<v Timothy Sini>我可以向你保证，这些不是萨福克县警察局的规则和程序。萨福克县警察局的规则和程序大约有5000页长。

00:39:28.360 --> 00:39:50.580
<v Hannah Dreier>They're 1,602 pages long. I have a copy. The Police Department's press office pointed out to me that if a child goes missing, the rules and procedures include another way they can find him or her. There are a few paragraphs about the statewide missing person alert system and how to request an alert. But the police never did that for Miguel, or the 15-year-old girl, or Oscar, or Jose.
<v Hannah Dreier>它们有1602页。我有一份副本。警察局新闻办公室向我指出，如果孩子失踪，规则和程序包括另一种他们可以找到他或她的方法。有几段关于全州失踪人员警报系统以及如何申请警报的内容。但警方从未为米格尔、那个15岁的女孩、奥斯卡或何塞做过这些。

00:39:50.580 --> 00:40:18.780
<v Hannah Dreier>The press office also said that police officers learn how to deal with missing children in more detail during their training. The rules and procedures also say that police officers have to provide free Spanish language interpretation for anyone whose primary language is not English. And the agreement with the Department of Justice says that police have to use interpretation services instead of forcing Spanish speakers to rely on their children or hire their taxi drivers. I asked Timothy Sini about the mom who told me she did just that.
<v Hannah Dreier>新闻办公室还表示，警官在培训期间更详细地学习了如何处理失踪儿童。规则和程序还规定，警官必须为任何主要语言不是英语的人提供免费的西班牙语口译服务。与司法部的协议也规定，警察必须使用口译服务，而不是强迫说西班牙语的人依靠他们的孩子或雇佣他们的出租车司机。我问蒂莫西·西尼关于那位告诉我她正是这样做的母亲的事。

00:40:18.780 --> 00:40:22.590
<v Timothy Sini>So we have a contract with Language Line, which is part of our language access plan.
<v Timothy Sini>所以我们与Language Line签了合同，这是我们语言访问计划的一部分。

00:40:22.590 --> 00:40:23.500
<v Hannah Dreier>Great, so I wonder why it wasn't used.
<v Hannah Dreier>太好了，所以我奇怪为什么没用它。

00:40:23.500 --> 00:40:30.550
<v Timothy Sini>Can you not interrupt me? So listen. So we have a contract with language access. If there is a--
<v Timothy Sini>你能别打断我吗？听着。所以我们有语言访问合同。如果有一个——

00:40:30.550 --> 00:40:57.580
<v Hannah Dreier>Language Line is a service where police can call an interpreter on the phone if there's no one available in the office. Police tell me it can be a hassle to use. Also, the connections can be bad, with dropped calls and annoyingly bad sound quality. I asked Former Commissioner Sini about the 15-year-old girl's experience, the one that was caught on the recording, and how many times the detectives refused to speak with her father in Spanish. But he said he wouldn't respond to, quote, "bald allegations."
<v Hannah Dreier>Language Line是一项服务，如果办公室里没人，警察可以通过电话呼叫翻译。警察告诉我使用它可能很麻烦。而且连接可能很差，通话中断，音质令人恼火地糟糕。我问前局长西尼关于15岁女孩的经历，就是录音里捕捉到的那个，以及侦探们多少次拒绝与她的父亲用西班牙语交谈。但他表示不会回应所谓“未经证实的指控”。

00:40:57.580 --> 00:41:26.410
<v Hannah Dreier>The Suffolk County Police tell me they've improved. They say they now have 10 certified interpreters, up from three. But according to the most recent DOJ report, from March, they're still not regularly using professional interpreters. The report says that when non-English speakers call the department, they are only given interpretation services 20% of the time. And the department is still letting officers use bilingual children as crime scene interpreters, even though the DOJ keeps saying that that should be banned.
<v Hannah Dreier>萨福克县警方告诉我他们已经改进了。他们说他们现在有10名认证翻译，高于之前的3名。但根据3月份最新的司法部报告，他们仍然没有经常使用专业翻译。报告称，当非英语母语者拨打该部门电话时，他们只有20%的时间得到口译服务。该部门仍然允许警官使用双语儿童作为犯罪现场翻译，尽管司法部不断表示那应该被禁止。

00:41:26.410 --> 00:41:50.830
<v Hannah Dreier>I recently talked to a 12-year-old girl whose brother was attacked by MS-13 in the woods by their house. The gang cut off his hand with a machete. When the police came to interview him, they asked her to interpret for him. She told me that after she heard her brother tell all the details of the attack, she didn't want to leave the house anymore.
<v Hannah Dreier>我最近和一名12岁的女孩谈过，她的兄弟在房子旁边的树林里遭到MS-13的袭击。帮派用砍刀砍下了他的手。当警察来采访他时，他们让她为他翻译。她告诉我，在她听到兄弟讲述袭击的所有细节后，她再也不想离开家了。

00:41:50.830 --> 00:42:06.430
<v Hannah Dreier>Everyone, former Commissioner Sini included, agrees on one thing-- what led to the boys being found, and their cases being investigated more rigorously-- were the next MS-13 attacks, the double murder of two teenage girls, Kayla Cuevas and Nisa Mickens.
<v Hannah Dreier>每个人，包括前局长西尼，在一件事上达成共识——导致男孩被找到、他们的案件得到更严格调查的是随后的MS-13袭击，即两名少女凯拉·奎瓦斯（Kayla Cuevas）和妮莎·米肯斯（Nisa Mickens）的双重谋杀案。

00:42:06.430 --> 00:42:35.320
<v Hannah Dreier>In September, seven months after Miguel disappeared, best friends Kayla and Nisa were walking on a quiet street near Kayla's home, when a group of boys they'd been feuding with at school jumped out of a car and attacked them. They used baseball bats and machetes. The attackers left 15-year-old Nisa's mangled body right there on the street. 16-year-old Kayla was missing overnight. The next day, a neighbor found her body in a patch of woods on the same block. Almost immediately, the full force of the Suffolk County Police Department kicked into gear.
<v Hannah Dreier>9月，在米格尔失踪七个月后，好朋友凯拉和妮莎正走在凯拉家附近一条安静的街道上，一群她们在学校一直有纠纷的男孩跳下一辆车攻击了她们。他们使用了棒球棍和砍刀。袭击者把15岁的妮莎那面目全非的尸体留在了街上。16岁的凯拉失踪了一夜。第二天，邻居在同一街区的树林里发现了她的尸体。几乎立刻，萨福克县警察局全力出动。

00:42:35.320 --> 00:42:52.930
<v Reporter>And Maurice, police are calling the murders of these two teenage girls an act of savagery on this community, and they're asking for the public's help. They're also stepping up anti-gang enforcement they have at every level, they say, patrolling this neighborhood and Brentwood High School to find the killer or killers.
<v Reporter>莫里斯，警方称这两名少女的谋杀案是对这个社区的残暴行为，他们正在寻求公众的帮助。他们也在各级加强反帮派执法，他们说，在这一社区和布伦特伍德高中巡逻以找到凶手或凶手们。

00:42:52.930 --> 00:43:16.750
<v Hannah Dreier>One obvious difference between the girl's deaths and the boys was that the police had the girls' bodies. The gang hadn't tried to hide them. But the boys' parents point out that the murders were different in another way. The boys were immigrants, from Spanish-speaking families. Kayla and Nisa, on the other hand, were girls born on Long Island, whose parents were English-speaking American citizens with nice homes and professional jobs.
<v Hannah Dreier>女孩的死和男孩的一个明显区别在于警方拥有女孩的尸体。帮派没有试图隐藏它们。但男孩的父母指出，谋杀案在另一个方面是不同的。男孩是移民，来自讲西班牙语的家庭。而凯拉和妮莎，是出生在长岛的女孩，她们的父母是讲英语的美国公民，有漂亮的房子和专业工作。

00:43:16.750 --> 00:43:43.990
<v Hannah Dreier>Dozens of officers went door to door, asking for tips. The department increased patrols and posted flyers offering a cash reward of $15,000 for help catching the girls' killers. Police arrested 25 suspected MS-13 members and mapped out their cliques. Within days, officers were searching the woods with German Shepherds and shovels. A Suffolk County Detective visited Carlota at home and swabbed her teeth for DNA. Timothy Sini held press conferences.
<v Hannah Dreier>数十名警察挨家挨户征求线索。该部门加强了巡逻，并张贴海报，悬赏1.5万美元帮助抓获杀害女孩的凶手。警方逮捕了25名嫌疑MS-13成员并绘制了他们的分支地图。几天之内，警官们就带着德国牧羊犬和铁锹搜查了树林。一名萨福克县侦探拜访了卡洛塔，提取了她的DNA样本。蒂莫西·西尼举行了新闻发布会。

00:43:43.990 --> 00:43:57.630
<v Timothy Sini>We've made a strategic subject list of known gang members in the area. And we're going to be enhancing our presence here to target those individuals.
<v Timothy Sini>我们制定了一份该地区已知帮派成员的战略目标清单。我们将在这里加强我们的存在，以打击那些个人。

00:43:57.630 --> 00:43:59.520
<v Hannah Dreier>Lady was watching all of this play out.
<v Hannah Dreier>莱迪一直在注视这一切发生。

00:43:59.520 --> 00:44:03.040
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:44:03.040 --> 00:44:08.370
<v Interpreter>When Miguel and Oscar went missing, barely anyone would talk about it.
<v Interpreter>当米格尔和奥斯卡失踪时，几乎没人谈论它。

00:44:08.370 --> 00:44:11.410
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:44:11.410 --> 00:44:18.240
<v Interpreter>But then, when the girls were murdered, then everyone was talking about it.
<v Interpreter>但后来，当女孩们被谋杀时，每个人都在谈论它。

00:44:18.240 --> 00:44:21.270
<v Lady>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Lady>[说西班牙语]

00:44:21.270 --> 00:44:27.160
<v Interpreter>But it was as though nobody could remember that there were some missing persons as well.
<v Interpreter>但似乎没人能记得还有一些失踪人员。

00:44:27.160 --> 00:44:30.830
<v Hannah Dreier>A few days after the girls were killed, Carlota was home watching Univision.
<v Hannah Dreier>在女孩们被杀几天后，卡洛塔在家里看Univision电视台。

00:44:30.830 --> 00:44:33.440
<v Reporter>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Reporter>[说西班牙语]

00:44:33.440 --> 00:44:38.400
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:44:38.400 --> 00:44:46.740
<v Interpreter>It was on TV that I learned when they found Oscar. And I just started to panic. Panic, panic, panic.
<v Interpreter>我是在电视上得知他们找到奥斯卡的。我开始恐慌。恐慌，恐慌，恐慌。

00:44:46.740 --> 00:44:51.150
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:44:51.150 --> 00:45:04.420
<v Interpreter>And my daughter said, no, Mom. But that's not Miguel. That's Oscar that they found. But I was panicking because I said, well, if they had found Oscar, then they practically found Miguel. And my daughter said, no, calm down. It's Oscar. It's not Miguel.
<v Interpreter>我的女儿说，不，妈妈。但那不是米格尔。他们找到的是奥斯卡。但我很恐慌，因为我说，好吧，如果他们找到了奥斯卡，那他们实际上就找到了米格尔。我的女儿说，不，冷静点。那是奥斯卡，不是米格尔。

00:45:04.420 --> 00:45:07.940
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:45:07.940 --> 00:45:17.270
<v Hannah Dreier>Police had found Oscar's body in the woods, the ones they call the killing fields, near some train tracks. The reporter also said that another body had been found in the same place.
<v Hannah Dreier>警方在树林里找到了奥斯卡的尸体，就是他们称之为杀戮场的地方，靠近一些铁轨。记者还说在同一个地方又发现了一具尸体。

00:45:17.270 --> 00:45:19.140
<v Reporter>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Reporter>[说西班牙语]

00:45:19.140 --> 00:45:25.160
<v Hannah Dreier>And just then, through the window of the basement apartment, Carlota saw two men in suits coming down the stairs.
<v Hannah Dreier>就在这时，通过地下室公寓的窗户，卡洛塔看到两个穿西装的人正走下楼梯。

00:45:25.160 --> 00:45:28.120
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:45:28.120 --> 00:45:38.720
<v Interpreter>I saw people coming in through the door. And when I saw that it was the same guy who came to take my DNA, I knew that they were here to give me some news.
<v Interpreter>我看到有人走进门。当我看到那是之前来提取我DNA的同一个人时，我就知道他们是来给我消息的。

00:45:38.720 --> 00:45:43.240
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:45:43.240 --> 00:46:01.490
<v Interpreter>And I just-- I don't know. I just started to scream, and cry, and kneel, and just like I was dragging myself on the floor, because I knew why they were there.
<v Interpreter>我只是——我不知道。我只是开始尖叫、哭泣、下跪，就像我在地板上拖着自己，因为我知道他们为什么在那里。

00:46:01.490 --> 00:46:04.290
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:46:04.290 --> 00:46:09.680
<v Interpreter>And then they left. And that's where I stopped remembering. It's like my mind was clouded.
<v Interpreter>然后他们离开了。我就在那停下了记忆。感觉我的心智被蒙蔽了。

00:46:09.680 --> 00:46:11.610
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:46:11.610 --> 00:46:15.630
<v Interpreter>And then, the next thing I knew, I woke up at the hospital.
<v Interpreter>然后，接下来的事我知道的就是我在医院醒来。

00:46:15.630 --> 00:46:32.020
<v Hannah Dreier>Carlota had run out of the apartment, tripped on some stairs outside, and smashed her face on the cement. The hospital staff wrote a summary on her chart. It said, quote, "altered mental state, patient repeatedly stating 'just kill me. My son, my son.'"
<v Hannah Dreier>卡洛塔跑出公寓，在外面楼梯上绊倒，脸撞在了水泥地上。医院工作人员在她的病历上写了一份摘要，引用：“意识状态改变，病人反复说‘杀了我吧。我的儿子，我的儿子。’”

00:46:32.020 --> 00:46:54.530
<v Hannah Dreier>When she was released from the hospital, she packed Miguel's things, his clothes, video games, and school papers, into five trash bags and put them on the street. She felt guilty for having brought Miguel to Brentwood in the first place, for having told him to ignore the kids who were bothering him in school. When he was missing, Miguel's things had felt like a vital connection to someone who was alive, but now they seemed like recriminations.
<v Hannah Dreier>当她从医院出院后，她把米格尔的东西，他的衣服、电子游戏和学校文件装进五个垃圾袋，扔到了街上。她感到愧疚，因为当初是她把米格尔带到了布伦特伍德，因为她告诉他在学校不要理会那些骚扰他的孩子。当他失踪时，米格尔的东西感觉像是与活着的人的重要联系，但现在它们看起来像是一种谴责。

00:46:54.530 --> 00:47:16.830
<v Hannah Dreier>The coroner listed the cause of death as a blow to the head. Within weeks of discovering Oscar and Miguel's bodies, police also found the body of Jose Pena, the third missing boy from Brentwood High School. He'd been buried in the same woods.
<v Hannah Dreier>验尸官将死因列为头部重击。在发现奥斯卡和米格尔尸体后的几周内，警方还发现了何塞·佩纳的尸体，第三名来自布伦特伍德高中的失踪男孩。他被埋在同一片树林里。

00:47:16.830 --> 00:47:28.800
<v Hannah Dreier>The double murder of Kayla Cuevas and Nisa Mickens and the police department's race to catch the killers got so much attention, it reached all the way to President Trump, who invited the girls' parents to his first State of the Union address.
<v Hannah Dreier>凯拉·奎瓦斯和妮莎·米肯斯的双重谋杀案以及警察局抓捕凶手的行动受到了极大的关注，甚至传到了特朗普总统那里，他邀请女孩的父母参加他的首次国情咨文演讲。

00:47:28.800 --> 00:47:50.550
<v Donald Trump>Their two teenaged daughters, Kayla Cuevas and Nisa Mickens, were close friends on Long Island. But in September 2016, on the eve of Nisa's 16th birthday-- such a happy time it should have been-- neither of them came home.
<v Donald Trump>她们两个十几岁的女儿，凯拉·奎瓦斯和妮莎·米肯斯，是长岛的好朋友。但在2016年9月，妮莎16岁生日的前夕——那本该是多么快乐的时光——两人都没能回家。

00:47:50.550 --> 00:47:54.900
<v Hannah Dreier>Trump drew a straight line from the girl's murders to the need for stronger immigration laws.
<v Hannah Dreier>特朗普直接将女孩们的谋杀案与加强移民法的必要性联系了起来。

00:47:54.900 --> 00:48:12.420
<v Donald Trump>Many of these gang members took advantage of glaring loopholes in our laws to enter the country as illegal, unaccompanied, alien minors, and wound up in Kayla and Nisa's high school.
<v Donald Trump>这些帮派成员中的许多人利用我们法律中的明显漏洞作为非法、无人陪伴的未成年外籍人士进入该国，并最终进了凯拉和妮莎的高中。

00:48:12.420 --> 00:48:29.540
<v Hannah Dreier>Of course, Miguel and Oscar and Jose were also in that high school. And their disappearances got so much less attention, not just by the president, but by the police. I asked former Commissioner Sini why the police seemed to do so little until the two girls died, especially if they thought the missing boys were homicides.
<v Hannah Dreier>当然，米格尔、奥斯卡和何塞也都在那所高中。但他们的失踪受到的关注少得多，不仅是总统，还有警方。我问前局长西尼为什么在两名女孩死亡之前，警方似乎做得那么少，特别是如果他们认为失踪的男孩是凶杀受害者。

00:48:29.540 --> 00:48:56.120
<v Timothy Sini>You know, when you have the double homicide of two high school girls in Brentwood, that's going to provide a catalyst to increase more resources in eradicating MS-13. And that's exactly what we did. If you want to criticize the Suffolk County Police Department for not doing enough against MS-13 prior to September 13, 2016, I suppose you can do that.
<v Timothy Sini>你知道，当你在布伦特伍德发生两名女高中生的双重凶杀案时，那将成为增加更多资源铲除MS-13的催化剂。这正是我们所做的。如果你想批评萨福克县警察局在2016年9月13日之前对MS-13做得不够，我想你可以这样做。

00:48:56.120 --> 00:48:57.620
<v Hannah Dreier>Why would you say that?
<v Hannah Dreier>你为什么那样说？

00:48:57.620 --> 00:49:10.660
<v Timothy Sini>Well, that's what you're suggesting. So what I'm suggesting to you is that it's clearly a fact that we did increase our efforts against MS-13 on September 13, 2016.
<v Timothy Sini>嗯，那是你的暗示。所以我向你暗示的是，这是一个明确的事实，我们在2016年9月13日确实增加了打击MS-13的力度。

00:49:10.660 --> 00:49:31.300
<v Hannah Dreier>It may be true that the police stepped up their efforts after September 13th, but they weren't necessarily effective. The murders continued. In October, a few weeks after the girls were killed, MS-13 killed another 15-year-old immigrant high schooler. When his father reported him missing, police listed him as a runaway. A few days later, a man's body was found in the street.
<v Hannah Dreier>警察在9月13日之后加强了工作力度可能是事实，但他们不一定有效。谋杀还在继续。10月，在女孩被杀几周后，MS-13又杀死了一名15岁的移民高中生。当他的父亲报失踪时，警方把他列为离家出走。几天后，一个男人的尸体在街上被发现。

00:49:31.300 --> 00:49:48.700
<v Hannah Dreier>After that, it was a suspected rival killed in a deli, then four young men cut up and left together in a gruesome tableau in the woods. All told, the gang killed eight more people by the spring of 2017. By then, MS-13 murders accounted for 40% of homicides in Suffolk County.
<v Hannah Dreier>在那之后，是另一个在熟食店被杀的敌对帮派成员，然后是四个年轻人被肢解并一起留在了树林里的一幅可怕景象中。总而言之，该帮派在2017年春天之前又杀了八个人。到那时，MS-13谋杀案占了萨福克县凶杀案的40%。

00:49:48.700 --> 00:50:05.320
<v Hannah Dreier>I've spoken with most of the victim's families. All of them are Latino immigrants. And even though their loved ones were killed after September 13th, they all told me they felt ignored and disrespected by the Suffolk County Police.
<v Hannah Dreier>我和大多数受害者的家人谈过。他们都是拉丁裔移民。尽管他们的亲人是在9月13日之后被杀的，但他们都告诉我他们感到被萨福克县警察忽视和不尊重。

00:50:05.320 --> 00:50:27.530
<v Hannah Dreier>There are lots of ways to frame why the cops treated them like this. The parents say it's because they're immigrants. The DOJ says the department has unlawful bias and discrimination. This one retired Suffolk County detective I talked to had another word for it. His name is Rob Trotta. He's now a county politician. He left the police department a year and a half before Timothy Sini came in.
<v Hannah Dreier>有很多方法可以解释警察为什么这样对待他们。父母说是因为他们是移民。司法部说该部门存在非法偏见和歧视。我交谈过的一位萨福克县退休侦探对它有另一个定义。他叫罗伯·特罗塔（Rob Trotta），他现在是县政客。他在蒂莫西·西尼上任前一年半离开了警察局。

00:50:27.530 --> 00:50:40.760
<v Rob Trotta>There is a term among law enforcement. It's called a misdemeanor murder. You know, they're killing each other. I guess it's a bad thing to say they were killing each other, but were killing low income, minority people. Which, you know, is horrible.
<v Rob Trotta>执法部门内部有一个术语。叫“轻罪谋杀”。你知道，他们互相残杀。我想说他们互相残杀是一件坏事，但杀的是低收入、少数族裔。这，你知道，是可怕的。

00:50:40.760 --> 00:50:47.010
<v Hannah Dreier>That means like a murder that's not going to get as much attention as the other ones, or you're not going to get as much glory for solving?
<v Hannah Dreier>这意味着像是不受关注的谋杀，或者你破案不会得到多少荣耀？

00:50:47.010 --> 00:51:08.360
<v Rob Trotta>No, it's like you know, you're anyone, and there's a murder where someone really got murdered, you're going to put attention to that, because this guy, you know, was-- I don't want to say asking for it. No one should be asking for murder, but clearly he put himself in a position to be killed. Now, that's very different from two girls being murdered, high school girls, by MS-13. That's not misdemeanor murder.
<v Rob Trotta>不，就像你知道，你是任何人，有一个人真的被谋杀的案子，你会关注它，因为这家伙，你知道，是——我不想说这是自找的。没人应该自找谋杀，但他显然把自己置于被杀的位置。现在，这与两个女孩被杀，女高中生，被MS-13谋杀是非常不同的。那不是轻罪谋杀。

00:51:08.360 --> 00:51:21.690
<v Hannah Dreier>I asked the Suffolk County Police if their cops use this term. They sent back a statement saying, quote, "Every investigation is rigorously conducted, regardless of victim or circumstances. And department resources are allocated accordingly."
<v Hannah Dreier>我问萨福克县警方他们的警察是否使用这个术语。他们发回一份声明称，引用：“每一次调查都是严格进行的，无论受害者或情况如何。部门资源也会相应分配。”

00:51:21.690 --> 00:51:40.910
<v Hannah Dreier>But there's another reason why the investigations into the boy's disappearances were so anemic. Several detectives and FBI agents talked to me about it. They said the team who handled cases like this in Suffolk County used to be excellent. This was over a decade ago, starting back in 2003, when the FBI first created a joint gang task force on Long Island.
<v Hannah Dreier>但调查男孩失踪案之所以如此无力，还有另一个原因。几位侦探和FBI特工和我谈过。他们说萨福克县处理这类案件的团队过去是非常优秀的。那是在十多年前，从2003年开始，当FBI首次在长岛建立联合帮派特别工作组时。

00:51:40.910 --> 00:51:56.570
<v Hannah Dreier>It's basically like a team made up of FBI agents and local detectives. They share intel, fight crime together. Suffolk County Police sent three of its detectives to join them. Rob Trotta was one of those detectives. And for nearly a decade, they had MS-13 on the run.
<v Hannah Dreier>它基本上是由FBI特工和当地侦探组成的团队。他们共享情报，共同打击犯罪。萨福克县警方派了三名侦探加入他们。罗伯·特罗塔就是其中一名侦探。近十年来，他们让MS-13处处受阻。

00:51:56.570 --> 00:52:14.000
<v Rob Trotta>I call it the 1978 Yankees, when everything just clicks and you know, we had informants everywhere. They really had a pulse for what was going on. We would cover gang and clique meetings, where we had two people wired up, just so we could see what was going on. If they broke off, we would have conversations and everything.
<v Rob Trotta>我称它为1978年的洋基队，当时一切都配合默契，你知道，我们到处都有线人。他们真正掌握了正在发生的事情的脉搏。我们覆盖帮派会议，我们在那两个人身上安装窃听器，只是为了看看正在发生什么。如果他们分开了，我们会进行对话等等。

00:52:14.000 --> 00:52:34.730
<v Hannah Dreier>They shared resources with the feds, who had way more money and training. They solved murders, and even got wind of planned murders before they happened and stopped them. It was going great, until, in 2012, the Suffolk County Police Chief at the time, a guy named James Burke, got into a squabble with the FBI and pulled his detectives from the task force.
<v Hannah Dreier>他们与联邦调查局共享资源，后者有更多的资金和培训。他们解决了谋杀案，甚至在谋杀发生前就得到了计划的消息并阻止了它们。一切都很顺利，直到2012年，当时的萨福克县警察局长，一个叫詹姆斯·伯克（James Burke）的人，与FBI发生了争执，把他的侦探从特遣部队撤了出来。

00:52:34.730 --> 00:52:53.400
<v Hannah Dreier>Then a few months later, Burke got into big trouble himself for beating up a guy who had been arrested for theft. The thief had stolen a duffel bag from Burke's car, which was weirdly filled with dildos and porn. The FBI started investigating him as a result. Burke was eventually put in prison for violating civil rights and obstructing justice.
<v Hannah Dreier>几个月后，伯克因为殴打一名因盗窃被捕的人自己惹上了大麻烦。小偷从伯克的车里偷了一个行李袋，里面奇怪地装满了假阳具和色情刊物。FBI因此开始调查他。伯克最终因侵犯民权和妨碍司法公正而入狱。

00:52:53.400 --> 00:53:19.290
<v Hannah Dreier>But one major casualty of the whole fiasco was the department's anti-gang capabilities. They were decimated. When Timothy Sini took over the police department in early 2016, he restored the partnership with the FBI. But by then, Suffolk County had lost out more than three years of MS-13 intel. And it was really hard to catch up.
<v Hannah Dreier>但整个闹剧的一个主要受害者是该部门的反帮派能力。它们被摧毁了。当蒂莫西·西尼在2016年初接管警察局时，他恢复了与FBI的合作关系。但到那时，萨福克县已经失去了超过三年的MS-13情报。而且真的很难赶上。

00:53:19.290 --> 00:53:38.880
<v Hannah Dreier>Finally, in March of 2017, a year after Miguel first went missing, the FBI gang task force, which now had Suffolk County cops back on it, made a big arrest. They charged two guys, 19-year-old Jairo Saenz and his 22-year-old brother Alexi, with organizing the murders of Kayla and Nisa six months before.
<v Hannah Dreier>终于，在2017年3月，米格尔失踪一年后，FBI帮派特别工作组，现在又有了萨福克县警察加入，进行了一次大逮捕。他们指控19岁的杰罗·萨恩斯和22岁的兄弟亚历克西在六个月前策划了凯拉和妮莎的谋杀。

00:53:38.880 --> 00:54:04.380
<v Hannah Dreier>The brothers were the leaders of the main MS-13 clique at Brentwood High School, the Sailors. They've pled not guilty. Federal prosecutors have now charged at least 28 people with participating in the MS-13 mayhem on Long Island. It's a massive, ongoing case that includes at least 15 murders, eight attempted murders, arsons, assault, racketeering, and conspiracies to sell marijuana and cocaine.
<v Hannah Dreier>两兄弟是布伦特伍德高中主要MS-13分支“水手（Sailors）”的头目。他们拒不认罪。联邦检察官现在指控至少28人参与了长岛的MS-13暴力事件。这是一个巨大的、正在进行的案件，包括至少15起谋杀案、8起谋杀未遂、纵火、殴打、敲诈勒索以及贩卖大麻和可卡因的阴谋。

00:54:04.380 --> 00:54:25.920
<v Hannah Dreier>Many of the accused ringleaders are teenagers. The case is still in progress, with new defendants added every few months. At least 10 people have been indicted for the murders of Oscar Acosta, Jose Pena, and the girls, Kayla Cuevas and Nisa Mickens. One person has already pled guilty to killing Jose Pena. But so far, no one's been charged for Miguel's death.
<v Hannah Dreier>许多被指控的头目都是青少年。此案仍在进行中，每隔几个月就会增加新的被告。至少有10人因奥斯卡·阿科斯塔、何塞·佩纳以及女孩们凯拉·奎瓦斯和妮莎·米肯斯的谋杀案被起诉。已经有一人承认杀害了何塞·佩纳。但到目前为止，还没有人因米格尔的死被起诉。

00:54:25.920 --> 00:54:34.220
<v Hannah Dreier>Carlota won't give Miguel a proper burial until his killer is brought to justice. So in the meantime, his ashes sit on a night stand.
<v Hannah Dreier>卡洛塔在凶手被绳之以法之前不会给米格尔进行正式葬礼。所以在此期间，他的骨灰放在床头柜上。

00:54:34.220 --> 00:54:35.300
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:54:35.300 --> 00:55:09.600
<v Interpreter>It's been three years, and the police have never come here to tell me anything. They just gave me back my son in a cardboard box. And that's what makes me mad. There are lots of parents, and we're all suffering. So if they could just have a little bit of wisdom, some feeling, some heart, to help us Latinos. I say Latinos, because if they were Americans-- and I'm sorry to say this, but if they were Americans, they would have found someone quickly. But because we're Latinos, we're always left behind.
<v Interpreter>已经三年了，警察从未到这里来告诉过我任何事。他们只是把我的儿子还给了我，在一个纸盒子里。而那正是让我愤怒的原因。有很多父母，我们都在受苦。所以如果他们能有一点智慧，一点情感，一点心，来帮助我们拉丁裔。我说拉丁裔，因为如果是美国人——我很抱歉这么说，但如果是美国人，他们会很快找到人的。但因为我们是拉丁裔，我们总是被抛在后面。

00:55:09.600 --> 00:55:15.650
<v Carlota>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Carlota>[说西班牙语]

00:55:15.650 --> 00:55:32.900
<v Hannah Dreier>I think I know what happened to Miguel. In those Facebook messages from the day he went missing, he and Alexander Lokote talk about meeting up with a guy named Jairo. Alexander wants to meet Miguel at Jairo's house. Jairo says don't bring a friend. Jairo is going to supply the weed. I'm waiting for you at the school with Jairo.
<v Hannah Dreier>我想我知道米格尔发生了什么。在失踪那天他发的那些Facebook信息中，他和亚历山大·洛科特谈到要和杰罗见面。亚历山大想在杰罗家见米格尔。杰罗说别带朋友。杰罗要提供大麻。我在学校和杰罗等你。

00:55:32.900 --> 00:55:50.930
<v Hannah Dreier>One of the leaders of the MS-13 clique, the Sailors, the guy who's been charged with six murders now, including Oscar's and Kayla's and Nisa's murders, is Jairo Saenz. This summer, I talked to a former member of Jairo's clique, a guy named Henry, who's in immigration detention right now.
<v Hannah Dreier>MS-13分支“水手”的头目之一，现在被指控六起谋杀案，包括奥斯卡和凯拉、妮莎谋杀案的家伙，是杰罗·萨恩斯。今年夏天，我谈到了杰罗帮派的一名前成员，一个叫亨利的家伙，他现在被关在移民拘留所。

00:55:50.930 --> 00:56:17.050
<v Hannah Dreier>Henry told me, after he joined the Sailors, the first person the clique killed was a chubby Ecuadorean kid. I showed him a picture of Miguel, and he said, yeah, that was him. Henry says Miguel confused the Sailors, because he wore a clashing mix of gang gear. Sometimes, he'd come to school wearing the plastic rosary of MS-13. Other times, he'd be in the head-to-toe black of the 18th Street Gang, or sporting a red bandanna, a sign of the bloods.
<v Hannah Dreier>亨利告诉我，在他加入“水手”后，帮派杀害的第一个人是一个胖乎乎的厄瓜多尔孩子。我给他看了米格尔的照片，他说，是的，那就是他。亨利说米格尔让“水手”很困惑，因为他穿的帮派装备乱七八糟。有时他戴着MS-13的塑料念珠去学校。有时他会全身穿黑色的“18街”帮派服装，或者是系着红头巾，那是“血帮”的标志。

00:56:17.050 --> 00:56:47.910
<v Hannah Dreier>Henry knew Miguel at school. Sometimes, they'd smoke weed together in the woods. And so one time, Henry says Jairo made him grill Miguel about why he was wearing clothes from these gangs, if he wasn't in a gang. Miguel said he didn't have to explain how he dressed to anyone. Henry thought Miguel was probably trying out gang clothes in an attempt to look cool. But the clique felt disrespected, and that's all it took to get Miguel killed-- a red bandanna.
<v Hannah Dreier>亨利在学校认识米格尔。有时他们会在树林里一起抽大麻。所以有一次，亨利说杰罗让他盘问米格尔为什么如果不是帮派成员却穿这些帮派的衣服。米格尔说他没义务向任何人解释自己的穿衣方式。亨利认为米格尔可能是在为了显得酷而尝试帮派服装。但帮派成员感到被不尊重了，而这就是导致米格尔被杀的全部原因——一条红头巾。

00:56:47.910 --> 00:56:57.300
<v Ira Glass>Hannah Dreier. She's a reporter for ProPublica. This story was a collaboration with them. There's a print version on their web site. ProPublica.org.
<v Ira Glass>汉娜·德雷尔。她是ProPublica的记者。这个故事是与他们合作的。他们的网站上有印刷版本。ProPublica.org。

00:56:57.300 --> 00:57:39.320
<v Ira Glass>Something upsetting recently happened on Long Island, that we thought we should point out before we leave this subject. One of the parents who we mentioned in this story, Evelyn Rodriguez, the mother of one of the girls who was killed, was also killed. Not by MS-13. It happened during an argument over the location of a memorial for her daughter. She was one of the parents who Donald Trump invited to the State of the Union address.
<v Ira Glass>最近长岛发生了一件令人不安的事，我们觉得在离开这个话题前应该指出来。我们在故事中提到的一位父母，伊芙琳·罗德里格斯（Evelyn Rodriguez），她是其中一名被杀女孩的母亲，也被杀了。不是被MS-13杀的。这发生在她女儿纪念碑位置的争执中。她是唐纳德·特朗普邀请参加国情咨文演讲的父母之一。

00:57:39.320 --> 00:57:55.890
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Dana Chivvis and Sean Cole. The people who put our show together today includes Elna Baker, Zoe Chace, Whitney Dangerfield, Damian Graef, Chana Joffe-Walt, Seth Lind, Lawrence Lowe, Anna Martin, Nadia Reiman, Lilly Sullivan, Christopher Swetala, Matt Tierney, and Dianne Wu. Our senior producer is Brian Reed. Our managing editor is Susan Burton.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由达娜·奇维斯（Dana Chivvis）和肖恩·科尔（Sean Cole）制作。今天整理我们节目的人包括埃尔纳·贝克（Elna Baker）、佐伊·蔡斯（Zoe Chace）、惠特尼·丹格菲尔德（Whitney Dangerfield）、达米安·格拉夫（Damian Graef）、查娜·乔菲-沃尔特（Chana Joffe-Walt）、塞思·林德（Seth Lind）、劳伦斯·洛（Lawrence Lowe）、安娜·马丁（Anna Martin）、纳迪亚·雷曼（Nadia Reiman）、莉莉·沙利文（Lilly Sullivan）、克里斯托弗·斯威塔拉（Christopher Swetala）、马特·蒂尔尼（Matt Tierney）和黛安·吴（Dianne Wu）。我们的高级制片人是布莱恩·里德（Brian Reed）。我们的总编辑是苏珊·伯顿（Susan Burton）。

00:57:55.890 --> 00:58:03.930
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks today to Peter Brill, Rob Bub, David Klinger, Vernon Geberth, Natalie Keyssar, Alexandra Zayas, Dan Golden, and our colleagues at ProPublica.
<v Ira Glass>今天特别感谢彼得·布里尔（Peter Brill）、罗伯·巴布（Rob Bub）、大卫·克林格（David Klinger）、弗农·格伯斯（Vernon Geberth）、娜塔莉·凯萨尔（Natalie Keyssar）、亚历山德拉·扎亚斯（Alexandra Zayas）、丹·戈尔登（Dan Golden）以及我们在ProPublica的同事。

00:58:03.930 --> 00:58:27.480
<v Ira Glass>Quick program note. Our program Serial just launched its third season. Sarah Koenig and Emmanuel Dzotsi host. I've been hearing drafts of this for months. I cannot recommend this highly enough. It's about the court system. It gives this very vivid picture of ordinary cases that turn out to be all kinds of incredible in all kinds of ways. You can hear it at serialpodcast.org, or wherever you get your podcasts.
<v Ira Glass>节目通知。我们的节目《Serial》刚刚推出了第三季。莎拉·凯尼格（Sarah Koenig）和伊曼纽尔·佐齐（Emmanuel Dzotsi）主持。我已经听了好几个月的草稿了。我怎么推荐都不为过。它是关于法院系统的。它展示了普通案件非常生动、在各方面都令人难以置信的景象。你可以在serialpodcast.org上收听，或者在任何你获取播客的地方。

00:58:27.480 --> 00:58:39.840
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is delivered in to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks as always our program's co-founder, Tory Malatia. He is still using Yahoo. He says to me all the time, when I try to look up something online--
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由公共广播交流平台PRX传送到公共广播电台。一如既往地感谢我们节目的联合创始人托里·马拉蒂亚（Tory Malatia）。他还在用雅虎。当我试图在线查东西时，他总是对我说——

00:58:39.840 --> 00:58:41.310
<v Timothy Sini>Well, don't Google it.
<v Timothy Sini>好吧，别谷歌。

00:58:41.310 --> 00:58:46.310
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周再回来，带来更多《美国生活》的故事。
